\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces CORE against cost. The curve $\mathrm  {CORE} = a + b\ln x$ is fitted to the three dense points. With three points and two parameters the fit has one residual degree of freedom, so its $R^2>0.999$ is close to automatic and is not itself evidence; the evidence is the agreement with the independently derived \textsc  {bpb}\ multipliers in the last column. ``Dense at MST's cost'' reads the fitted curve at MST $L{=}24$'s value; the multiplier is the dense cost needed to reach MST's CORE, divided by MST's actual cost, so $>1$ favours MST. The final column repeats the \textsc  {bpb}\ multipliers of Table~\ref {tab:ratio} for comparison.}}{11}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:coreladder}{{6}{11}{CORE against cost. The curve $\mathrm {CORE} = a + b\ln x$ is fitted to the three dense points. With three points and two parameters the fit has one residual degree of freedom, so its $R^2>0.999$ is close to automatic and is not itself evidence; the evidence is the agreement with the independently derived \bpb \ multipliers in the last column. ``Dense at MST's cost'' reads the fitted curve at MST $L{=}24$'s value; the multiplier is the dense cost needed to reach MST's CORE, divided by MST's actual cost, so $>1$ favours MST. The final column repeats the \bpb \ multipliers of Table~\ref {tab:ratio} for comparison}{table.6}{}}
